Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: qcom: preserve SM8475 divider during testbus writes

SM8475 uses UFS_TEST_BUS_CTRL_2 bit 4 as a live HCI turbo clock divider.
The generic UTP_HCI testbus selector overlaps that bit: error dumps sweep
all 32 selectors and change the divider on half of the writes. Restoring
the final value cannot prevent transient timing changes during recovery.

Exclude bit 4 from the UTP_HCI write mask on the SM8475 controller. Also
match SM8475 machines whose controller retains the SM8450 compatible, as
on Asphalt. ufshcd_rmwl masks the selector value with the write mask, so
the divider remains unchanged on every register update, in both turbo
states. Keep the complete dump traversal and other controllers unchanged.

diff --git a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -2044,6 +2044,17 @@
 	case TSTBUS_UTP_HCI:
 		reg = UFS_TEST_BUS_CTRL_2;
 		offset = 0;
+		/*
+		 * SM8475 reuses bit 4 as the HCI turbo clock divider. Keep it
+		 * out of every testbus write, including error-dump traversal.
+		 * Older SM8475 trees identify the controller as SM8450.
+		 */
+		if (of_device_is_compatible(host->hba->dev->of_node,
+					   "qcom,sm8475-ufshc") ||
+		    (of_machine_is_compatible("qcom,sm8475") &&
+		     of_device_is_compatible(host->hba->dev->of_node,
+					     "qcom,sm8450-ufshc")))
+			mask &= ~BIT(4);
 		break;
 	case TSTBUS_UNIPRO:
 		reg = UFS_UNIPRO_CFG;
